+### A run that ends in its prewarm loses what it compiled
+
+- **Symptom:** every start compiles the same few dozen to few hundred bases again, and area loads stall on them (a draw waits in `pso_get` → `arch_capture` → `MTLCompiler`), although each run compiled them before. The run's last lines say `pipeline archive at exit: 425 pipelines not written yet, not all written in time: those left are built again at the next start (968 ms)`, or `[RED] graceful exit not done in 1.5 seconds, exiting at once` with no archive line at all.
+- **Cause:** since [A cold start leaves a file per timer flush](#a-cold-start-leaves-a-file-per-timer-flush-and-the-next-start-compacts-them-all), the timer's flushes wait for the prewarm's end, and a period is written only when full (`ARCH_PERIOD_MAX`, 2,000) or at exit. Ocarina of Time's prewarm (31,663 lines after the first frame, on 5 workers) outlives a play session, so everything a session compiled waited for the exit. The graceful exit gave the archive what was left of 1.5 s, while writing takes 1.8–4 s per 900 bases. When a draw was still compiling its pipeline, `dm_gfx_suspend` waited on that draw, and the 1.5 s fallback ended the process before any write. A test harness that stops a run for "no new frame" sends `SIGTERM` exactly then.
+- **Fix:** during a prewarm that is not compacting, the timer's flush hands the period over once it is `DM_PSO_ARCH_WARM_FLUSH_MS` old (60 s by default, `0` turns it off). Each later one waits twice as long, up to 16 times, so a cold start adds about one file per doubling, not one per 10 s. While captures wait, the timer looks again every 10 s. In the graceful exit (`hle_lifecycle.c`), the archive starts writing first (`dm_gfx_quit_begin`), before the drain, and gets `DM_EXIT_ARCH_MS` (8 s, or 3 s on iOS) beyond the 1.5 s. If graphics are not suspended within 1.5 s, the fallback waits for the archive's write inside the same deadline; only one of the two paths writes and ends the process. A draw that is still compiling its pipeline holds its period open, so that period is written only if the compile returns within the budget; the periods before it are written either way. A hard deadline at 1.5 s plus the archive's budget plus 0.5 s ends the process whatever happens (2.0 s with `DM_EXIT_ARCH_MS=0`, whose fallback then hands the archive over without waiting). Files are still written as `.tmp` and renamed after their index, so a write the deadline cuts short leaves the files already there valid; a start removes a `.mtl4a.tmp` a stopped write left, and an index whose file never came.
+- **Check:** `test_pso_archive.py`, in fresh processes. Five new bases in a prewarm, then the timer's flush after `DM_PSO_ARCH_WARM_FLUSH_MS=300`, then `_exit`: one file, and the next start takes all five and compiles none (before: no file, five compiled). `SIGTERM` in a prewarm, with and without a draw that holds the graphics layer (`dm_gfx_enter` with no leave): one file, all five taken at the next start (before: none with the draw held). A torn write's `.tmp` and an index without its file, beside a file that is not an archive: the leftovers are removed, the bad file is skipped, and all five bases come from the good file (before: the `.tmp` stays).
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) on an M4 Pro (macOS 26.6): every warm-up run ended with 400–500 bases not written, and the next runs compiled 57–167 bases and missed the archive 57–256 times. Hyrule Field, Zora's Domain and Kakariko stalled for more than 20 s at the load.
+
 ### Archive lookups asked every file, so a few files made a start compile every base again
 
 - **Symptom:** a start after some play spends minutes in the prepare step and the log says `pipeline archive compacted: N files of bases with a vertex layout gave M bases to the files just written and were removed`: Ocarina of Time on an M4, 9 files of play beside a compaction's 5 made the next start capture 8,543 bases again, 713 s before the first frame (PR #1880); after a cold start's 80 files, 762 s ([A cold start leaves a file per timer flush](#a-cold-start-leaves-a-file-per-timer-flush-and-the-next-start-compacts-them-all), [issue #1747](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1747)).
